Table~\ref{tab:oracle} reports the results. All three oracle variants achieve \emph{lower} CAR than both unrefined baselines. The best oracle (Oracle-feature, 76.9\%) falls 5.1 points below LoRA (82.0\%) and 6.1 points below Full-FT (83.0\%). Oracle-adaptive and Oracle-fixed perform worse still at 75.0\% and 73.1\%. Refinement with perfect distance information produces more collisions than doing nothing. Fake safe rates confirm that oracle refinement does not reduce action collapse: Oracle-adaptive and Oracle-fixed both register 98.3\% (118/120 and 115/117 safe episodes), Oracle-feature registers 96.7\% (119/123). Ground-truth gradients redirect near-zero motion into near-zero motion in a different direction; they cannot generate viable trajectories from a collapsed policy.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_oracle_bar.pdf}
\caption{Aggregate CAR for oracle SDF refinement variants versus unrefined baselines. All three GT oracle variants achieve lower CAR than both baselines, despite access to perfect distance information.}
\label{fig:oracle_bar}
\end{figure}

The per-task breakdown reveals why refinement backfires on active episodes. On task~1 (\texttt{chocolate\_pudding}), where the EE is the primary collision body and the LoRA baseline achieves only 46.0\% CAR, Oracle-adaptive improves to 55.0\% and Oracle-feature to 47.5\%. This is the expected case: EE-directed refinement helps when the EE itself is the collision site. On the remaining tasks, all oracle variants degrade performance. Oracle-adaptive reduces Task~0 (\texttt{orange\_juice}) from 96.0\% to 82.5\% and Task~2 (\texttt{milk}) from 98.0\% to 77.5\%. The SDF gradient pushes the EE away from the nearest surface, but the resulting joint-space displacement rotates intermediate arm links toward or through obstacle volumes. Refinement trades one collision for another with no mechanism to detect the substitution.

The feature-space oracle makes this coupling explicit. It applies the SDF gradient in the policy's latent space rather than in Cartesian coordinates, and fires on nearly every timestep (241.7 refinements per episode vs.\ 14.2 for Cartesian variants). Yet gradient magnitudes are two orders of magnitude smaller (0.004--0.006 vs.\ 0.18--0.49 in action space), and CAR (76.9\%) shows no advantage over action-space oracles despite 17$\times$ more steps. The latent space lacks geometric grounding: small perturbations in feature space do not translate into collision-free joint configurations.

The A3 configuration with a learned SDF head illustrates a complementary failure mode. Across all 160 evaluation episodes, the SDF network never outputs a distance below the refinement threshold, so zero refinement steps occur. The learned decoder has calibrated its outputs to avoid triggering the safety mechanism, and the refinement module sits inert. The resulting CAR of 74.5\% is consistent with the oracle range, confirming that the model operates as an unrefined baseline.

These results establish that EE-only SDF refinement is a structural limitation, not an engineering gap. Perfect distance information cannot compensate for the absence of full-body collision awareness. Any scheme that monitors only the EE position remains blind to arm-body collisions, which dominate in manipulation tasks where the robot must reach around obstacles. Effective collision avoidance for VLA models requires geometric representations that span the entire manipulator, not only its tip. Section~\ref{sec:avoidance_source} investigates what does produce collision avoidance in fine-tuned models and how geometric supervision contributes.

\section{Avoidance Source Diagnosis}\label{sec:avoidance_source}

